#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175749 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "6a090caa-4c73-5f90-9cb1-dc7dcdfe9e37" :question/math-academy-id "e-8906" :question/problem "Consider the segment of the line $y = 2x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this segment is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x\\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175750 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "6ee942e8-9fb9-5dfb-8f9c-aea1dd6d53ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8907" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^2$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown below. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/30c2b0000055802166e5bdf8ec3e305d83e65ac960043f66463702952d91e4d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175765 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "d0546997-c726-5ec2-ae10-c2373ed8b863" :question/math-academy-id "e-9121" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x^3-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/777c36689da6f6cbcf4ce92a7c5ba17c000a7c442a972851d9d1cafaa9fb2ba4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x= \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-3x)=3{x}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+(3{x}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}-18{x}^{2}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a749ded0-2fb8-5d57-a65d-979e2ef8edd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin x\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173443} {:answer/id #uuid "f402d949-5cd4-56b1-bd76-5a119f45abc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{0}^{π/2}x\\cos^{2} xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173444} {:answer/id #uuid "0538f271-9c1d-52ab-af5c-03d48163d2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin x\\sqrt{1 + \\sin^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173445} {:answer/id #uuid "077afecb-b76b-5b22-822e-052d0c159b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{0}^{π/2}x\\sin^{2} xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173446} {:answer/id #uuid "630b4aa9-ffa2-5990-95cf-0ed4bfbf7ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/2}x\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "630b4aa9-ffa2-5990-95cf-0ed4bfbf7ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/2}x\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173447} :answer-field/id #uuid "132765ce-c248-5a65-a076-6cabdd57f346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ddd5046-3b26-5239-932f-f9bb69f6472e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104518" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9abd4c99-7d93-5498-b343-a3bbbb32bffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173449} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c7d233-35d8-54c7-aaa3-5912147aee81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173450} {:answer/id #uuid "1a493100-54e0-50d6-bbd1-ca545f0c2313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173451} {:answer/id #uuid "6255cab6-bb85-5d88-9772-514c804db2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173452} {:answer/id #uuid "6e90b79a-a490-562c-b181-72777dd63014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c7d233-35d8-54c7-aaa3-5912147aee81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173450} :answer-field/id #uuid "f11e6216-0812-56b9-abbc-da7fcf04d9ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c05778a-860e-50f5-bcb4-539729cfbaeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104529" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y}{2\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi \\,y{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c861f554-d898-5e76-90fa-330abe831192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387179} {:answer/id #uuid "00520f55-4bf3-5694-ba03-a5d373d8d96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4230f9-0ed1-5e41-a369-2fef50a5b75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387181} {:answer/id #uuid "6108cb78-bb5e-58f6-8fe1-1f29295c8b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387182} {:answer/id #uuid "3e93f3d1-a196-5cfb-85f9-7ee88ab1d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6108cb78-bb5e-58f6-8fe1-1f29295c8b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387182} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a0b2b7-29a1-5e2c-88af-78b72cd8fee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58c515e6-dbc9-5e2a-acb7-4ae381a18c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-109237" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/2baa8f39370b831bd8306aa3eb60e5d16d54ce50474f873f0fa329c069d61689.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies between $y=1$ and $y=e,$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46d6b07-c504-57c2-9f88-6b03a5ecd116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{4}(3 + 2y - y^{2})\\sqrt{4y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173593} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd0438b-2539-5f64-9e20-90ee860143ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{-1}^{3}y\\sqrt{4y^{2} - 8y + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173594} {:answer/id #uuid "25bd8386-a4d8-5961-9fe6-f75ffe5df14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{4}\\sqrt{4y^{2} - 8y + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173595} {:answer/id #uuid "42a6a06d-42c5-55f4-be89-92be993ea69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{3}(1 - y)\\sqrt{4y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173596} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcc0fb2-24ad-5ac1-aa58-f36e3437462a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{3}(3 + 2y - y^{2})\\sqrt{4y^{2} - 8y + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fcc0fb2-24ad-5ac1-aa58-f36e3437462a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{3}(3 + 2y - y^{2})\\sqrt{4y^{2} - 8y + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173597} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d2875d-a914-58c7-89a2-b2210a37c626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65178d09-dde4-5078-bae9-d7478c40486a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109253" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/12d30907e2fb6cdd031134a667c7de1324fec66025c0f2029b18f4521e94da83.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=3+2y-{y}^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y-3 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3+2y-{y}^{2})=2-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}-8y+4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3881748-5c50-584d-acd2-28a17c0ad4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-2y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387186} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecaec5a-0d7f-5b85-84fe-2f9e982bf2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387187} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea68a15-7001-5b69-ab88-6bf65be04dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387188} {:answer/id #uuid "6532a921-7fea-5e87-973c-0531a31714d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387189} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d9ce2a-8f22-5c9f-854e-89dd07bc91a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-4)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6532a921-7fea-5e87-973c-0531a31714d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387189} :answer-field/id #uuid "356b928a-f168-5aae-bf5c-de994cab5562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3a98f8f-e2c5-5fe4-a763-31a2ca07130e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109258" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/3d01ddb2278875780397e90a9d6f6ad6b2fc44890c59faab7a69b8ebcb5d210b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=4-(y-4)^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-(y-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=6.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4-(y-4)^{2})=8-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(4-(y-4)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(8-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b302ff15-c1f7-51c3-b651-b19250f131a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387193} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fa417d-66ae-51e0-8669-8b320b43f56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387194} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3720d8-e838-5615-b913-b130824fb4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387195} {:answer/id #uuid "22eba780-b2ca-53b9-be8a-d45de50a6808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387196} {:answer/id #uuid "04552900-9000-58d5-b3f8-9d76d9a911ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0fa417d-66ae-51e0-8669-8b320b43f56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387194} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed22e06-904d-5d92-a07f-2c0dbe381873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bee3dde-bc52-5306-8b97-caa53ff33030" :question/math-academy-id "q-109261" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8403e013-7cee-5dbc-bd5c-3486b5c8ca6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173623} {:answer/id #uuid "b4776486-5df2-5547-b428-698b241e034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{116π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173624} {:answer/id #uuid "05a2672b-c386-5865-bcfb-b0943f5131ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{232π}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173625} {:answer/id #uuid "10d943b0-7437-587c-a229-4d2427068f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{201π}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173626} {:answer/id #uuid "7d61074a-7477-5c98-8a11-8f2c200964af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05a2672b-c386-5865-bcfb-b0943f5131ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{232π}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173625} :answer-field/id #uuid "7478fb12-8884-5838-a3ee-f9ca95a659c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca31b615-018c-5f6b-b173-7f397965d43a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109413" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2})={x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=x.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{2(4)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-(\\frac{2(1)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{116}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{232\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b207a69d-4d85-5560-826b-642021578a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi \\sqrt[3]{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387200} {:answer/id #uuid "63f6f98d-7175-5b60-ad1a-7a3aec351e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387201} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6392b6-fca6-5949-a216-7c009590ac95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387202} {:answer/id #uuid "71a59533-649b-5d91-8a27-4f9162b5b1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387203} {:answer/id #uuid "931e159e-fab9-5f02-9c4a-e1e82b238a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f6f98d-7175-5b60-ad1a-7a3aec351e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387201} :answer-field/id #uuid "94571208-ec13-5b20-bda6-923048467fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bc19c87-6706-5df0-8c49-f02af32af50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109415" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y={(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{1/2}(-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/3}{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}={x}^{-2/3}(4-{x}^{2/3})=4{x}^{-2/3}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{-2/3}-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{-2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {[\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b23462b9-6e6a-5b7b-b2ae-2dc8b1d4cd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387207} {:answer/id #uuid "d096dad1-f2a4-5b85-9340-014022dcb096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387208} {:answer/id #uuid "45729e87-95e2-5d84-b3ab-9df6b271145a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387209} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf3b8c2-d98d-5981-824c-413384bef8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387210} {:answer/id #uuid "912948a4-ba42-5249-b69d-6603fdefc6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-3x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45729e87-95e2-5d84-b3ab-9df6b271145a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387209} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0fcbd5-0315-5e89-aaea-23bf20b00696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f62024cb-fdce-53f3-b058-afb4afce16ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-109418" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/f70f186e51a312b26dc4e2cfa3a4d812fab6583ada8b8af82f153a9975948b4b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x)=2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+{(2x-3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88cf1bf7-9bae-5fd3-ac8e-26d588695123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387214} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e7f0af-1c4a-5c10-bbcc-be0a9a8479d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5cb24c-20de-57d6-b730-0c78a6a0a232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{4-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387216} {:answer/id #uuid "c55a2487-082b-5340-b298-150089224ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{12-5{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} {:answer/id #uuid "c2096800-1d98-5370-b94f-74087a96247b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{4-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88cf1bf7-9bae-5fd3-ac8e-26d588695123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387214} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a7915a-ba9f-5b5c-a5d0-afced806b268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c20af06-f742-5d0f-a68e-3d8868398167" :question/math-academy-id "q-109420" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/21d8b516e09e592bd77c815ba50b10faed648386013f9d0a618df5ec758b9a91.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=5x-{x}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(5-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-{x}^{3})=5-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{1+{(5-3{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfe25412-aca3-52ce-bb04-b89c67e7ed75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387221} {:answer/id #uuid "8a44d7f3-bbd7-5a4a-a5c5-8c675db4d021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387222} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1934bf-2016-5008-b0cc-a0077ed316e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387223} {:answer/id #uuid "7b91a85b-e310-5180-a96c-d697f2407821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387224} {:answer/id #uuid "b0508f1b-bb41-503f-9caa-b4b5857f0b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a44d7f3-bbd7-5a4a-a5c5-8c675db4d021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387222} :answer-field/id #uuid "7737d57c-a033-549d-8c34-3c3ece78ebcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2f2469e-26cc-5373-b28c-d53223eec629" :question/math-academy-id "q-126610" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/aa927160974aa22e09f1a8c0b47d588a63f22fc2dd018c3bba36d4a00c649435.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bec4232-6c73-5939-a1e8-8ac3d0dfab87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1-y)}^{2}\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387228} {:answer/id #uuid "df071846-265f-5e7b-969f-4008b36ee11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387229} {:answer/id #uuid "dafdbaf9-8aee-5b0b-a710-bd03f9c6d673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387230} {:answer/id #uuid "d0316645-8395-54ae-864d-8433236e7125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387231} {:answer/id #uuid "0a83360b-0d84-5897-b17c-d907e07c09b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a83360b-0d84-5897-b17c-d907e07c09b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387232} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b89eac6-b9ab-5277-b118-d44214515dd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e80b22b7-d962-5938-af93-f2a5f86bbdff" :question/math-academy-id "q-126615" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/ae26451a8f2962ed873fc5eb703197375bc1a02eff2240bd21cebac81ef26205.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})=1-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(1-y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(1-2y+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d5f09cd-5729-5c47-a05a-11c75c7807fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387235} {:answer/id #uuid "334b6284-3f1f-5823-a2d1-4eac5dadc478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387236} {:answer/id #uuid "6744aee8-7138-5a4a-bd63-338733a357ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387237} {:answer/id #uuid "c486d335-9233-5c80-9e60-39fae11f7f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387238} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6e1fdc-2408-53c8-aee4-5ccbcbfb3f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d5f09cd-5729-5c47-a05a-11c75c7807fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387235} :answer-field/id #uuid "d497aea2-9b5f-57c4-8de0-b75d780c782d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcb08d10-5de4-50f1-acf0-54b821666e7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126715" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/bbdb10a20dd90cd0928e91851662fccaf8d0b03e86c44f58b5311ba805183e8e.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x+3$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4x+3)=2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+{(2x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20797785-32e4-5473-b653-54ee0b13660d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387242} {:answer/id #uuid "741818e3-f923-5151-899e-b84a557211a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387243} {:answer/id #uuid "853ad045-c621-5a92-bc48-87219614fd25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387244} {:answer/id #uuid "be6483c6-5e1c-547d-a90c-e407e4b139b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387245} {:answer/id #uuid "0739faf7-dff8-544c-901c-028c60753eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0739faf7-dff8-544c-901c-028c60753eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387246} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b38dc9-25bc-5057-a2c1-310aed9d4497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdbf3732-d9db-5aa9-97e3-008429e155d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126731" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt[4]{3}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}{y}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{y}^{4}.$ Then, ${y}^{3}\\text{d}y=(\\frac{1}{4})\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}{y}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db26732-f885-507c-9d0c-db8fbff8f515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db26732-f885-507c-9d0c-db8fbff8f515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387249} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba67a48c-6d26-5da6-a7f3-9708b4bd01af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05455a71-2acd-54af-8ede-155df1478797" :question/math-academy-id "q-353019" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=1+{x}^{2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+{x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+4{x}^{2}.$ Then, $8x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(2)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
